00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:17.220
Joe, please give us your vote.

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:28.820
Well, alright, first of all, I want to, I want to talk about something that, that I kept bringing up, which is the fact that you, there's no feedback when you attack.

00:43:29.380 --> 00:43:37.500
And I am all for, in games, especially in RPGs, for things that make you have to use your imagination.

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:42.960
Like, you know, thinking of, like, more, more classic Final Fantasy type games.

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:45.180
I think that's what half the charm of some of those comes from.

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:50.640
Even, like, so I've been playing a lot of Final Fantasy lately, so that's what's got this on my mind.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:57.100
But, you know, even, like, before there was voice acting in those games, I think a lot of the charm came from imagining that.

00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:07.440
Or, like, a lot of the, you know, I can sit down and play a game where there's three characters lined up and one of them runs up and swings their sword at them and another one shoots a gun.

00:44:07.500 --> 00:44:12.920
And I can believe that, like, okay, this is a realistic battle because there's a little bit of imagination to be had.

00:44:13.120 --> 00:44:15.900
And I think that, like, a lot of RPGs rely on that.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:20.940
What Heidlite, I believe, does wrong is not the fact that you can't see it.

00:44:21.020 --> 00:44:26.280
I don't think there's anything inherently wrong with, like, that sort of feedback where it's, like, just telling you you're hitting.

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:38.840
But when you're doing an action game, you need the feedback in order to, in order to, not in order to be immersed, but in order to, in order to actually understand what's happening.

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:42.260
Actually be able to react to things because things are happening in real time.

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:52.880
So I wanted to specify that because I do actually have a pretty strong stance in, like, for some of these older games that are, like, turn-based or that are using outdated forms.

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:56.540
So that is why I'm so hard on it here.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:58.940
I don't know if this is making any sense or if it sounds relevant at all.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:00.260
But that's...

00:45:01.720 --> 00:45:02.860
No, I get what you're saying.

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:04.700
Yeah. In any other...

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:11.140
Like, there are genres where you're not going to literally swing a sword or press the button to swing a sword.

00:45:11.340 --> 00:45:14.140
Like, combat just happens differently.

00:45:14.940 --> 00:45:23.000
Exactly, yeah. And that is, to me, a big problem with this game because of the reason that, like, you actually can't react to things properly.

00:45:23.320 --> 00:45:26.120
But, like we talked about, there's plenty of other problems to this game.

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:32.540
Especially, like, the fact that there's nothing to do in it other than grind so you can play the game, which almost is like a self-defeating thing.

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:38.660
I think it's really cool that they kind of got these systems in place.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:43.820
I think it, at the time, probably meant good things for the future of this genre.

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:49.840
But we're seeing this game on the NES several years after it was released originally.

00:45:49.840 --> 00:45:53.360
So I think it's just out of place, outdated, not essential.

00:45:53.680 --> 00:45:54.740
All right, Jim.

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:57.780
Yeah, one thing we...

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:03.360
I kept trying to get us to mention, but we never did mention, is that you play as Jim the Knight.

00:46:03.940 --> 00:46:08.620
Which I think really makes me like this game more.

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:12.220
But, yeah, this game...

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:14.440
I've said it before.

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:17.080
It basically just systemizes...

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:21.200
It streamlines any interaction with the game out of it.

00:46:21.200 --> 00:46:29.220
So that all you're doing is walking around and bumping into shit and finding boxes and getting killed by bees.

00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:33.080
It's so out there.

00:46:33.340 --> 00:46:36.420
Everything about it seems so counterintuitive in the way it's designed.

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:42.300
It's definitely a curio.

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:51.940
I think that this is a game to be studied for how many poor decisions went into designing the game mechanics.

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:53.640
But in no...

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:56.500
I can't recommend anyone actually play it.

00:46:56.780 --> 00:46:58.260
So, no, it's not essential.

00:46:58.260 --> 00:46:59.600
Yeah, that's where I'm at.

00:46:59.740 --> 00:47:00.960
It's the prototype, right?

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.740
Like, this is the start of something.

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:11.800
And, you know, I think even if you go back to, like, the first of any genre, right?

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:14.400
And, again, we're not saying this is officially the first or whatever.

00:47:14.440 --> 00:47:19.400
But when you go back to the beginnings of any genre, there's always the refinement, right?

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:25.520
I can't think of any video game genre that hasn't been improved upon as time goes on.

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:31.300
And, like, yeah, you could argue, like, Super Mario Brothers is an early platformer.

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:32.920
It's not, like, one of the first.

00:47:33.020 --> 00:47:34.140
But, yeah, it's an early platformer.

00:47:34.180 --> 00:47:36.280
And it nails it. And it's still considered good today.

00:47:36.480 --> 00:47:41.880
Sure. But, like, I don't think anyone's going to say that Heidlite nailed it.

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:43.320
I think Heidlite had an idea.

00:47:43.900 --> 00:47:48.080
And then later people figured out better ways to execute on that.

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:51.300
So that's why it's not going on the Essential Games list.

